<section><div class="wide"><h2 id="configuration-inputs">Where values come from</h2>
<p>A factory captures loaded configuration. Java builders contribute choices for a particular Mailer or Email. A supplied Jakarta Mail Session
  brings its existing connection setup. Email defaults and overrides contribute message values when the Email is completed or prepared.</p>
<p>Source loading happens first. With an explicit <code>ConfigLoader</code>, later non-blank source entries win; blank or null entries do not erase an earlier value.
  <code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()</code> loads the conventional classpath file, then environment variables, then JVM system properties on its first call.
  <a href="/spring.html">Spring</a> supplies its own Environment precedence, profiles and placeholder handling. A file value replaced by an environment value
  for the same property is already resolved before later Java choices are considered.</p>
<p>The captured snapshot does not change when you edit its original Properties or process settings. Create a new factory and fresh Mailers to use new configuration;
  see <a href="/configuration.html#section-config-snapshot">replacing a configuration snapshot</a>. A CLI daemon captures startup settings once and needs a restart to reload them.</p>
</div></section>
<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h2 id="configuration-resolution-timing">When choices take effect</h2>
      <p>Supplying a value and applying templates are separate steps. A setter records your choice at its documented scope; it can still check
        its arguments or perform documented work such as reading signing material. The operation that completes that configuration combines
        the applicable choices:</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it completes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ConfigLoader.load()</code></td><td>Reads the ordered sources and creates the factory's configuration snapshot.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>buildMailer()</code></td><td>Combines and checks choices for this Mailer and the named failover Mailers it creates, then constructs them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>buildEmail()</code></td><td>Creates the submitted Email and performs its documented construction work. Ordinary Email defaults and overrides remain unapplied.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides(...)</code></td><td>Explicitly applies the chosen Email governance during this call.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sending a composed Email</td><td>Applies the selected destination's Email defaults, overrides and locks during preparation for that send.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mailer.rehearse(...)</code> and <code>mailer.validate(...)</code></td><td>Use the invoked Mailer's own configuration. They do not select or validate a future cluster or failover destination.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Conversion and export</td><td>Follow the governance chosen for that conversion. Fluent <code>fromEmail(...)</code> export does not implicitly apply factory Email templates; exporting a rehearsal uses its already-prepared snapshot.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>These configuration checks run before starting workers, proxy bridges or pools. Later builder edits do not change the captured choices.
        Some checks also need the actual runtime state, such as whether a Session is already in use or a shared pool has incompatible settings.
        If setup fails, Simple Java Mail cleans up the resources it started for that attempt.</p>
      <pre><code class="language-java">Email submitted = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Draft subject")
    .withPlainText("Draft body")
    .buildEmail();
// Subject and body still contain the submitted text.

mailer.sync().sendMail(submitted);
// The selected Mailer's applicable Email templates are applied during preparation.</code></pre>
      <p>Subject and body use the same single-value template precedence. Recipients, headers and complete policies keep their
        <a href="#configuration-precedence">field-specific rules</a>. Connection settings also follow the
        <a href="#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>; that policy does not change message-template, pool or execution precedence.
        <a href="features.html#section-exact-eml">Exact Email</a> preparation retains its separate byte-preservation contract.</p>
    </div></section>
<section><div class="wide">
			<h2 id="configuration-precedence">When values replace or combine</h2>
			<table><thead><tr><th>Scope or value</th><th>Resolution</th></tr></thead><tbody>
				<tr><td>Property sources</td><td>Later non-blank values win. Blank or null entries do not erase an earlier value.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ordinary Mailer settings, such as pool size or legacy content support</td><td>Explicit Java choice, then captured configuration, then the published library fallback. Omitted group members inherit independently.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Supported connection settings</td><td>Different explicit declarations fail in strict mode. Deliberate override mode gives explicit typed Java members precedence over raw counterparts; omitted members retain applicable values.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Supplied Session gaps</td><td>Ordinary factory connection defaults leave omitted Session properties absent. Deliberate customization or applicable locks can add settings.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Email scalars and complete policy values</td><td>Applicable override, then submitted Email, then applicable default. Signing and encryption configurations are selected as a whole.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Recipients, Reply-To, delivery-recipient overrides, attachments and embedded images</td><td>Add missing override entries, retain every submitted entry, then add missing default entries. Submitted duplicates and their order remain intact.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Headers</td><td>Combine names case-insensitively. For one name, an override replaces its entire value list; otherwise submitted values replace the default list.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Recipient certificates and NOTIFY preferences</td><td>Group policy constructs the recipient metadata. The resulting explicit recipient value wins over the governance-resolved Email fallback, including an Email override.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Configuration locks</td><td>Separate mandatory restrictions remain binding through ordinary clears, template replacement and suppression.</td></tr>
			</tbody></table>
			<p><a href="/configuration.html#email-template-exclusions">Email template exclusions</a> determine which ordinary template contributions apply. Sending, rehearsing and exporting the original exact Email
				preserve its final bytes without ordinary templates or security transformations. Compatible envelope locks still apply;
				locks that would require changing the preserved content are rejected.
				See the <a href="security.html#section-sending-smime">recipient certificate rules</a> for the separate group-policy choices.</p>
		</div><h3>A subject, recipients and headers use different rules</h3>
<p>This example starts with an empty factory configuration so the three contributions are visible. Completing the Email selects one subject,
  combines recipient contributions and selects a complete value list for the matching header name.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(ConfigLoader.builder().load());
Email defaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Default").to("audit@example.org")
    .withHeader("X-Route", "default").buildEmail();
Email overrides = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Override").to("compliance@example.org")
    .withHeaders(Map.of("x-route", List.of("override-1", "override-2"))).buildEmail();
Email submitted = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withSubject("Submitted").to("alice@example.org").to("alice@example.org")
    .withHeader("X-ROUTE", "submitted").buildEmail();

try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder().withTransportModeLoggingOnly(true)
        .withEmailDefaults(defaults).withEmailOverrides(overrides).buildMailer()) {
    Email completed = mailer.getEmailGovernance().produceEmailApplyingDefaultsAndOverrides(submitted);
    // Subject: Override
    // To: compliance, alice, alice, audit (all at example.org)
    // X-Route, matched case-insensitively: [override-1, override-2]
}</code></pre>
<p>An empty submitted subject is still a supplied value and prevents the default from filling it. An eligible override can replace it.
  Clearing the local subject allows ordinary template fallback again. Complete signing/encryption configurations are selected together;
  their key, domain or other members are not assembled from different Email templates.</p><h3>Calendar choices and template exclusions</h3><p>A calendar's method and text belong together. Excluding either <code>CALENDAR_METHOD</code> or <code>CALENDAR_TEXT</code>
				skips that source's whole calendar. An override exclusion lets the Email's own calendar, or otherwise its default calendar, apply;
				a default exclusion leaves overrides eligible. Your Email's own calendar remains. If you excluded both selectors, allowing only one
				leaves the other exclusion active. Calendar transfer encoding is a separate choice.</p><div class="wide">
			<h3 id="repeated-email-completion">Completing an Email more than once</h3>
			<p>A completed Email can be completed or sent again, including through another Mailer. Each pass can fill missing scalar values and apply
				the selected Mailer's overrides. Completion keeps your suppression choices and always enforces applicable locks. It does not freeze the message.</p>
			<p>Completion carries outgoing content and settings, including S/MIME, OpenPGP and DKIM configuration; protection is applied later when MIME
				is prepared. Historical cryptographic results and original protected bytes, <a href="delivery-reports.html#trust-and-preservation">received delivery reports</a>
				and their nested facts, decrypted-attachment inspection lists and retained S/MIME
				signed-content views are not copied into the completed Email. Inspect the original converted Email for those results. Ordinary copying and
				supported Java serialization preserve input history. Content already present in ordinary bodies, headers and resources remains outgoing content.</p>
			<p>Additive templates contribute only values not already present. For example, supplied recipients <code>[A, A, B]</code>, overrides
				<code>[A, C, C]</code> and defaults <code>[B, D, D]</code> produce <code>[C, A, A, B, D]</code>. Reapplying those templates keeps that
				order. Recipient matching includes the name, address, To/Cc/Bcc role, certificate and notification preferences, accounting for missing
				names supplied by mandatory locks. Explicit conflicting names still fail validation.</p>
			<p>Attachments and images match only when their metadata and recognized source agree. Reusing the same resource or DataSource is recognized;
				library-owned paths and byte arrays can also be compared without opening a stream. Different filenames, descriptions, Content-IDs or
				encoding choices remain distinct. Duplicate detection does not read files or remote content. Different opaque sources remain separate,
				even if their names and MIME types match; serialization or reconstruction can lose recognizable source identity.</p>
			<p>For ordinary sends, use <code>buildEmail()</code> and let the selected destination apply its settings. If you send a completed result,
				its materialized values are now supplied values: another Mailer's default sender cannot replace an existing sender, but an override can.
				Headers and complete policies retain the rules above. The original exact Email and frozen retry content keep their separate preservation contracts.</p>
		</div><p class="wide">
			Explicitly calling <code>mailer.getEmailGovernance().produceEmailApplyingDefaultsAndOverrides(exactEmail)</code> applies ordinary defaults and
			overrides to the parsed view and returns a new composed Email. The supplied exact Email remains unchanged. The composed result does not guarantee
			the original EML bytes or the validity of signatures already on the source; reuse the original exact Email when those bytes must be preserved.
		</p><p class="wide">
			A template can also supply the message being forwarded. A default fills a missing forwarded message; an override replaces the one supplied
			on the Email. Use <code>EmailProperty.MAIL_TO_FORWARD</code> with the per-field opt-outs below to skip that template contribution.
			The subject still follows its own precedence. The selected <code>MimeMessage</code> remains borrowed, so keep it available and unchanged
			while the Email is being used; applying templates does not read or copy its body. Sending, rehearsing and exporting an exact Email still bypass ordinary templates.
		</p></section>
<section><div class="wide">
      <h2 id="configuration-operations">Changing an existing choice</h2>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Meaning at the documented scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><code>withX(value)</code></td><td>Supply a choice. Group setters replace the supplied group; collection methods can append where their contract says so.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>clearX()</code></td><td>Remove that scope's choice. Another fallback can apply; some clears explicitly remove a captured setting too.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>resetX()</code></td><td>Select the published built-in fallback, including absence when that is the fallback.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>useConfiguredX()</code></td><td>Restore captured configuration for that member or group.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Builder group getters</td><td>Inspect supplied choices, including absence and explicit-clear flags, without resolving or validating runtime settings or allocating resources. This includes <code>getMailSendRetryPolicy()</code>; use the built Mailer's <code>OperationalConfig</code> for resolved retry settings.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Built Mailer configuration getters</td><td>Inspect the effective construction settings. They do not track later direct Session mutations.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <p>Not every setting has every operation. The setter's contract defines its scope, and configuration locks still apply.
        For example, <code>resetMaxSize()</code> chooses the library pool maximum, while <code>useConfiguredMaxSize()</code> restores the captured maximum.
        Neither changes the other supplied members.</p>
      <p>Suppose the factory captured <code>simplejavamail.defaults.connectionpool.maxsize=7</code>. Replacing the pool group discards its previous
        supplied maximum and claim timeout:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-java">ConnectionPoolConfig firstPoolChoices = ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
    .withMaxSize(9)
    .withClaimTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(firstPoolChoices);

mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(ConnectionPoolConfig.builder()
    .withCoreSize(1)
    .build());
// Supplied max size and claim timeout are now null.
// The built Mailer resolves max size to the captured 7.</code></pre>
      <p>To make the same core-size change while retaining the original supplied maximum and claim timeout, edit that value instead:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(firstPoolChoices.toBuilder()
    .withCoreSize(1)
    .build());
// Supplied max size remains 9 and claim timeout remains two seconds.</code></pre>
      <p>Use <code>mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()</code> when editing the builder's current group.
        These pool-group edits leave previously built values and Mailers unchanged. For a caller-owned Session, removing an explicit connection choice restores that
        Session's baseline where applicable; it does not start applying ordinary factory connection defaults.</p>
      <p>Clearing creation-age expiration explicitly disables even a captured age setting. Restoring inheritance is a separate operation;
        both retain the group's other choices, including idle expiration:</p>
      <pre><code class="language-java">ConnectionPoolConfig noCreationAge = mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()
    .clearExpireAfterCreation()
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(noCreationAge);
// getExpireAfterCreation() is null; isExpireAfterCreationCleared() is true.

ConnectionPoolConfig inheritedCreationAge = noCreationAge.toBuilder()
    .useConfiguredExpireAfterCreation()
    .build();
mailerBuilder.withConnectionPoolConfig(inheritedCreationAge);
// getExpireAfterCreation() is still null; isExpireAfterCreationCleared() is false.
// The built Mailer can use the factory's captured age setting again.</code></pre>
    </div><p>Each configuration-group setter replaces that group's supplied choices. Omitted fields use remaining applicable configuration rather than
        keeping the previous group's values. The connection conflict policy remains separate from group replacement.
        Use <code>mailerBuilder.getConnectionPoolConfig().toBuilder()</code>, for example, to retain choices while editing a group, or
        <code>useConfiguredConnectionPoolConfig()</code> to restore inheritance for the whole group. Group builders own detailed inspection and
        clear/reset methods; root getters inspect supplied groups. Effective settings remain available on the built Mailer.</p><p>An explicit empty Java string remains a supplied choice and follows that setting's validation or clearing rules.
        It does not restore a captured value. This differs from blank entries in property sources, which are treated as absent.
        Use the named clear or <code>useConfigured...</code> operation to express either intent explicitly.</p><p id="explicit-connection-clears">In the unreleased 10.0.0 line, an explicit hostname or local-bind clear can remove the corresponding
        unlocked direct Jakarta Mail property, including a captured raw extra-property overlay. Removing an imported value is a conflicting
        customization under <code>REJECT_CONFLICTS</code>; deliberate removal requires <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>.
        <code>clearLocalBindAddress()</code> removes
        both address and port; the member-specific port clear removes only the port. Unset choices inherit, <code>useConfigured...</code>
        restores captured configuration, and resets select library defaults. Mandatory locks and caller-Session inherited-property limits still apply.</p><h3>Editing Email templates</h3><p class="wide">
			Repeated <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code> calls replace the template. <code>useConfiguredEmailDefaults()</code> restores the snapshot-derived
			defaults. <code>withoutEmailDefaults()</code> omits ordinary message defaults while retaining configuration locks;
        <a href="/configuration.html#email-template-exclusions">per-email exclusions</a> are a separate choice.
		</p><p class="wide">
			To remove only default DKIM signing while preserving other property defaults, materialize the defaults, copy that Email, call
			<code>clearDkim()</code>, and pass the result to <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code>. Materialization still reads any configured signing key.
			The <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#dkim-defaults">migration example</a> shows both replacing and removing the default signature.
		</p><div class="wide">
			<p>Clearing a submitted Email's local DKIM choice has a different scope: ordinary templates can supply signing again.
				Exclude those template contributions separately when that is intended:</p>
			<pre><code class="language-java">EmailPopulatingBuilder draft = mail.emailBuilder()
    .copying(original)
    .clearDkim();
// The local choice is gone; ordinary signing defaults or overrides can still apply.

draft.withTemplateOptions(draft.getTemplateOptions().toBuilder()
    .dontApplyDefaultValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
    .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(EmailProperty.DKIM_SIGNING_CONFIG)
    .build());</code></pre>
			<p>Editing the existing options retains unrelated exclusions. This suppresses ordinary DKIM template contributions for that Email;
				it does not remove mandatory configuration locks or change the original Email.</p>
		</div><p>On <code>EmailTemplateOptions.Builder</code>, repeated suppression calls accumulate exclusions; an empty call changes nothing. Use <code>allowDefaultValueFor(...)</code>
				or <code>allowOverrideValueFor(...)</code> to remove selected exclusions, and <code>clearDefaultValueSuppressions()</code>
				or <code>clearOverrideValueSuppressions()</code> to remove all exclusions in that family. These edits leave global
				<code>ignoringDefaults()</code>/<code>ignoringOverrides()</code> choices and configuration locks unchanged.</p></section>
<a href="#connection-conflicts" id="connection-conflicts" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Connection authority and restrictions</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>In the unreleased 10.0.0 line, <code>ConnectionConflictPolicy.REJECT_CONFLICTS</code> is the default. If factory configuration,
        recognized Jakarta Mail properties, a supplied Session and explicit Java choices declare different values for the same supported
        connection setting, <code>buildMailer()</code> fails before changing the Session or opening a connection. The error identifies the
        setting and available source labels. Equivalent parsed values are accepted; built-in defaults are not competing declarations.
        Trusted-host lists with the same exact names are compatible even when their order or repeated names differ. Factory objects are
        compared by identity, and empty text is kept distinct from an omitted setting.</p>
      <p>Property sources resolve first. A later environment value replacing the same property from a file is ordinary loading, including in
        Spring. The policy checks the resulting declarations against each other, such as an SJM proxy host and a different raw SOCKS host.</p>
      <p>Select <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code> when the application deliberately replaces supplied connection setup. Explicit typed Java
        members then win over their raw counterparts regardless of call order. Unspecified members retain applicable raw values: the example
        selects <code>chosen-proxy.example.org:1081</code>, rather than replacing the raw port with a proxy default.</p>
      <p>A raw SOCKS host can include a decimal port, such as <code>proxy.example.org:1081</code>. Without a separate port, a malformed inline
        suffix keeps the provider's default port. A separate raw SOCKS port takes precedence and keeps its numeric-property formats, including hexadecimal.
        Adding SJM proxy credentials preserves that interpreted remote endpoint. An untouched supplied Session keeps an omitted port;
        an ordinary factory port does not silently replace the provider's fallback.</p>
      <p>Only String property names participate in Simple Java Mail's connection resolution. Non-String keys in a supplied <code>Properties</code> object remain opaque
        entries. For supported text properties, inspection and locks use the inherited String that Jakarta Mail sees, even behind a direct
        non-String value. Inherited socket-factory class names remain subject to the same TLS-lock restrictions as direct entries.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withConnectionConflictPolicy(
        ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES)
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.socks.host", "shared-proxy.example.org")
    .withProperty("mail.smtp.socks.port", 1081)
    .withProxyConfig(SocksProxyConfig.builder()
        .withHost("chosen-proxy.example.org")
        .build())
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
      <p>Without the policy selection, the different proxy hosts conflict. In either mode, compatible raw host/port values can combine with
        SJM proxy credentials. The selected remote route is resolved before authenticated proxying uses its local bridge.</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Default; Java can select a different mode unless it is locked:
simplejavamail.connection.conflictpolicy=REJECT_CONFLICTS

# Alternatively, require strict handling within this factory:
simplejavamail.locked.connection.conflictpolicy=REJECT_CONFLICTS</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <p>This advanced policy covers supported SMTP, proxy, socket-timeout, local-binding, EHLO and TLS connection settings.
        It does not change Email defaults, execution, pooling or telemetry rules, or interpret arbitrary provider extensions and hidden
        Authenticator state. <a href="#locked-requirements">Locks</a> and mandatory TLS requirements remain binding in both modes.</p>
      <p>Repeated setters still replace earlier local choices, and replacing a group discards its previous supplied choices. The policy compares
        the completed choices, not setter history. Clearing an imported connection value is a conflict in strict mode; clearing a local-only
        choice remains normal builder editing. See <a href="#configuration-operations">clearing, resetting and restoring configuration</a>.</p>
      <p><code>getConnectionConflictPolicy()</code> on the builder inspects its explicit selection. <code>resetConnectionConflictPolicy()</code>
        selects <code>REJECT_CONFLICTS</code>; <code>useConfiguredConnectionConflictPolicy()</code> restores the captured policy, or the strict
        default if none was configured. Inspect the resolved mode with <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getConnectionConflictPolicy()</code>,
        or read the captured setting with <code>ConfigKeys.CONNECTION_CONFLICT_POLICY</code>. The CLI accepts
        <code>--mailer:withConnectionConflictPolicy ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code>, and Spring accepts the same property values.</p>
    </div>
  </section><section><div class="wide"><h3>One configuration group can contain different rules</h3>
<p><code>ConnectionConfig</code> groups related API choices, but the conflict policy applies to supported connection declarations.
  Its legacy-content flag keeps ordinary Mailer precedence. Here, Java's legacy-content choice is valid in strict mode, while the different timeout conflicts.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig deployment = ConfigLoader.builder().withMap("deployment", Map.of(
    "simplejavamail.defaults.readtimeoutmillis", 10_000,
    "simplejavamail.smtp.legacycontentsupport", true)).load();
SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(deployment);
ConnectionConfig choices = ConnectionConfig.builder()
    .withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(20))
    .withLegacySmtpContentSupport(false).build();
MailerRegularBuilder&lt;?&gt; builder = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 25).withConnectionConfig(choices);
// builder.buildMailer() would reject the different read timeout in strict mode.

builder.withConnectionConflictPolicy(ConnectionConflictPolicy.ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES);
try (Mailer mailer = builder.buildMailer()) {
    // Read timeout: 20 seconds. Legacy content support: false.
}</code></pre>
<p>If the Java value only supplied <code>withLegacySmtpContentSupport(false)</code>, construction would succeed in strict mode
  and retain the configured ten-second read timeout. Applicable locks constrain both examples.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="wide"><h3 id="supplied-sessions">A supplied Session keeps its baseline</h3><p>A supplied Session keeps its connection settings, including settings it leaves absent. Ordinary factory connection defaults do not
        replace its timeouts, local binding, EHLO name or certificate trust, or fill their gaps. Deliberate customization and applicable locks
        can add settings. Different explicit declarations of the same setting fail under the default
        <a href="#connection-conflicts">connection conflict policy</a>; select <code>ALLOW_EXPLICIT_OVERRIDES</code> when replacement is intended.
        This is independent of whether the Session previously belonged to another Mailer.</p><p>If a setting comes from an inherited <code>Properties</code> default, clearing it cannot remove that default. Such a clear is rejected
        before changing the Session. Supply a replacement value or create a new Session with independent Properties containing only the
        settings you want.</p>
<p>Reuse its Mailer for concurrent sends. A different Mailer cannot claim that Session or its shared Properties until the current owner has shut down successfully.
  Configure custom SSL factories and opportunistic TLS directly on the supplied Session; use an SJM-created Session when SJM should own those construction choices.</p>
</div></section><section>
    <h3 id="locked-requirements">What locks protect</h3>
    <p class="wide">A lock prevents accidental changes within this factory. It is not a security sandbox: application code can deliberately create
      an independently configured factory or use another transport. It also cannot guarantee final delivery, an arriving DSN, or downstream compliance.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Exact EML keeps its bytes. Compatible envelope additions, such as an archive BCC, are allowed; a lock that would require changing preserved
        content is rejected. Matching readable headers can satisfy a lock without rewriting them. Duplicate Subject, From, or Reply-To fields make a
        lock on that field ambiguous and are rejected; a single Reply-To field can still contain multiple addresses. Existing opaque signatures are
        not treated as proof that a required signing configuration was used.</li>
      <li>Clusters check the selected SMTP configuration as well as the invoking Mailer's restrictions. A different destination cannot silently weaken
        them. Ordinary sends, batches, and open-connection sends all retain the message requirements.</li>
      <li>A caller-owned Session must agree with the applicable locks. If the SMTP username or password is locked, use this factory's
        <code>mailerBuilder()</code> without supplying a Session. Even a matching visible username cannot rule out cached credentials or an
        Authenticator choosing another account. Custom TLS handling is also rejected when it prevents checking the applicable locks.
        Do not mutate a Mailer's Session while it is in use.</li>
      <li><code>CustomMailer</code> receives the governed message, but owns its transport and delivery envelope. Locks on those settings, including archive
        recipients, are rejected with that callback. Content settings that Simple Java Mail prepares still apply.</li>
      <li>Locked DSN requests require explicit provider support and an SMTP server advertising DSN. A third-party adapter cannot silently fall back to
        best-effort behavior. Connection TLS and onward REQUIRETLS remain separate requirements.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
<section><div class="wide"><h2 id="configuration-diagnostics">Tracing an unexpected result</h2>
<p>First check which source supplied the loaded value, then whether a later Java choice or Email template applies. The factory's
  <code>mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> describes its captured properties and their sources. It is not a combined report of every builder,
  supplied Session or runtime pool. The built Mailer's <code>getOperationalConfig()</code> reports its resolved construction settings.</p><p><code>ConnectionConfig</code> and its builder inspect supplied timeout choices, including unset values. On the built Mailer,
        <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getConnectTimeout()</code>, <code>getReadTimeout()</code> and <code>getWriteTimeout()</code>
        report effective construction settings. <code>getSessionTimeout()</code> returns their common <code>Duration</code> only when all three
        are known and equal, otherwise <code>null</code>. Null does not mean unlimited, and inspection does not guess provider defaults or track
        direct Session mutations after construction. <code>ConnectionConfig.getSessionTimeout()</code> follows the same equality rule for its supplied choices.</p>
<p>Builder getters inspect supplied choices. They do not complete the configuration or allocate connection resources. Each public setter's Javadoc
  defines its setting-specific validation and clearing behavior. Use the
  <a href="/debugging.html#section-config-diagnostics">configuration diagnostics guide</a> for safe logging and worked source-tracing examples.
  Do not log ordinary configuration maps or getters containing real credentials.</p>
<p>Invalid source values fail during loading; unsupported combinations and conflicting connection choices fail during construction.
  Connection conflict errors identify the setting and available declaration sources. Align or remove an obsolete declaration, or deliberately choose the connection override policy when that is intended.
  Locks and transport requirements remain mandatory. Unknown provider extensions and hidden authentication state keep their provider-defined behavior.</p>
</div></section>
